  <title>Virtual machines</title>
  <p>A virtual machine is a brick that QEMU runs. Its settings have a section for each part of it: <gui>Machine</gui>, the QEMU program, the type of machine, its processors and its memory; <gui>Disks</gui>; <gui>CD-ROM and boot</gui>; <gui>Network</gui>; <gui>Display</gui>; <gui>Sound and USB</gui>; <gui>Kernel</gui>, to boot a kernel of your own; and <gui>Advanced</gui>.</p>
  <p>The line at the top says which QEMU will run the machine, and its version. The lists of machine types, CPU models, sound cards and network cards are what that QEMU has. When the program isn't there, the line says so, and the lists have only the choices of the machine. A setting that this QEMU lacks says so under it, in the colour of warnings, and its section is marked at the left: the machine starts without it, and the <gui>Logs</gui> window says so.</p>
  <p>The settings take effect when the machine starts again.</p>
  <section id="machine">
    <title>Machine</title>
    <figure>
      <title>The section Machine</title>
      <media type="image" mime="image/png" src="figures/machine-settings.png">
        <p>The settings of vm1: the line at the top names its QEMU, the sections are at the left, and the section Machine shows Program, Machine type, CPU model, KVM, Virtual CPUs and Memory.</p>
      </media>
    </figure>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Program</gui></title>
        <p>The QEMU program, by its architecture, as <sys>x86_64</sys> for <cmd>qemu-system-x86_64</cmd>: the list has those in the QEMU folder of the project, see <link xref="programs#where"/>. One that isn't there shows as not installed. <sys>i386</sys> by default.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Machine type</gui></title>
        <p>The type of machine that QEMU emulates, as <sys>q35</sys> or <sys>virt</sys>; <gui>The default</gui> for the one of QEMU, which the list names. The programs of ARM, and that of RISC-V in QEMU 11, have no default: choose one, as <sys>virt</sys>, or the machine can't start.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>CPU model</gui></title>
        <p>The processor that QEMU emulates; <gui>The default</gui> for the one of QEMU.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>KVM</gui></title>
        <p>Run the machine with KVM, the virtualization of the processor of this computer, far faster than emulating it. Where KVM can't run, as without <file>/dev/kvm</file> or for a processor of another kind, QEMU emulates it all the same. Off by default.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Virtual CPUs</gui></title>
        <p>The number of processors of the machine, from 1 to 64; 1 by default.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Memory</gui></title>
        <p>The memory of the machine, in MiB, from 1 to 99999; 64 by default.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>KVM shadow memory</gui>, <gui>Shadow memory</gui></title>
        <p>With <gui>KVM</gui>: the memory that KVM keeps for the page tables of the machine, in MiB, when the default of QEMU isn't enough. Off by default.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>ACPI</gui></title>
        <p>Power management. <gui>Stop</gui> asks the system of the machine to shut down through ACPI: without it, the system doesn't hear it, and only <gui>Terminate</gui> or <gui>Kill</gui> stop the machine. On by default.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
  </section>
  <section id="disks">
    <title>Disks</title>
    <figure>
      <title>The section Disks</title>
      <media type="image" mime="image/png" src="figures/machine-disks.png">
        <p>The section Disks of vm1: the disk hda with the image debian, qcow2, a disk of 1.0 GB, used as a Private copy, and the line under it, vm1's changes will be kept in vm1_hda.cow, made at the next start; debian stays as it is. Add Disk under it, then Changes: Forget the changes and Virtio disks, off.</p>
      </media>
    </figure>
    <p><gui>Add Disk</gui> adds a disk on the first free device: <sys>hda</sys> to <sys>hdd</sys>, the floppies <sys>fda</sys> and <sys>fdb</sys>, and <sys>mtdblock</sys>. Each disk chooses its image in a list of the images of the project, which says what each is and who uses it. The list also has <gui>Add an Existing Image…</gui>, <gui>New Empty Disk…</gui> and <gui>Manage Images…</gui>, which shows the <gui>Images</gui> tab.</p>
    <terms>
      <title>How a disk uses its image</title>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Private copy</gui></title>
        <p>The default. The machine writes its changes to a file of its own in the folder of the project, made at its next start, as <file>vm1_hda.cow</file>, and the image stays as it is. Several machines can share one image this way.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>The image itself</gui></title>
        <p>The machine writes to the image.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
    <p>The line under a disk says what it does. The menu of a disk has <gui>Save as a New Image…</gui>, which keeps the changes of the machine as an image of their own; <gui>Merge into</gui> the image, which writes them into it, and lists first what else uses it; <gui>Start Over from</gui> the image, which throws the changes away; <gui>Show in Files</gui> and <gui>Remove Disk</gui>. Saving and merging run in the background, with their progress, and can be stopped.</p>
    <terms>
      <title>Changes</title>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Forget the changes</gui></title>
        <p>The machine writes the changes to its disks in temporary files, forgotten when it stops: each start begins from the same disks. Off by default.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Virtio disks</gui></title>
        <p>Attach the disks as virtio devices, faster than those of a PC, for a system that has their driver, as Linux, which names them <sys>vda</sys>, <sys>vdb</sys> and so on. Off by default.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
  </section>
  <section id="cdrom">
    <title>CD-ROM and boot</title>
    <figure>
      <title>The section CD-ROM and boot</title>
      <media type="image" mime="image/png" src="figures/machine-cdrom.png">
        <p>The section CD-ROM and boot of vm1: CD-ROM, Nothing, so Image and Drive are greyed; Boot from, The default.</p>
      </media>
    </figure>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>CD-ROM</gui></title>
        <p>What the CD-ROM holds: <gui>Nothing</gui>, the default; <gui>An image</gui>, a file as an <file>.iso</file>; or <gui>A drive</gui> of this computer.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Image</gui></title>
        <p>With <gui>An image</gui>: the image file. Type it, or choose it with the button beside it.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Drive</gui></title>
        <p>With <gui>A drive</gui>: the CD-ROM drive of this computer, as <file>/dev/cdrom</file>.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Boot from</gui></title>
        <p>The first place the machine boots from: <gui>The default</gui>, that of QEMU; <gui>The first disk</gui>; <gui>The CD-ROM</gui>; <gui>The floppy</gui>; or <gui>The network</gui>.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
  </section>
  <section id="network">
    <title>Network cards</title>
    <figure>
      <title>The section Network</title>
      <media type="image" mime="image/png" src="figures/machine-network.png">
        <p>The section Network of vm1: the card eth0, with the button that removes it; Model, rtl8139; MAC address, 52:54:00:12:34:01, with the button that makes another; Plugged into, sw1. Add Card under it.</p>
      </media>
    </figure>
    <p><gui>Add Card</gui> adds a card after the others. The cards are in the order the machine sees them, the first one first, <sys>eth0</sys> in the settings. The button at the end of a card removes it. Each card has:</p>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Model</gui></title>
        <p>The network card that QEMU emulates, of those that this QEMU has; <sys>rtl8139</sys> by default, or another one where QEMU lacks it.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>MAC address</gui></title>
        <p>The address of the card on the network. A new card has a random one; the button beside it makes another.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Plugged into</gui></title>
        <p>What the card joins:</p>
        <list>
          <item>
            <p>a switch of the lab, by its name;</p>
          </item>
          <item>
            <p><gui>The host only, on QEMU's user network</gui>: QEMU joins the machine to this computer, and through it to the networks this computer reaches, with no switch;</p>
          </item>
          <item>
            <p><gui>Other bricks plug into it</gui>: the card is a socket, and a wire can plug into it when the setting <gui>Plugs into machines</gui> of the project allows it, see <link xref="settings#project"/>;</p>
          </item>
          <item>
            <p><gui>Nothing</gui>: the card goes nowhere, and the machine can't start until you plug it in or remove it.</p>
          </item>
        </list>
      </item>
    </terms>
  </section>
  <section id="display">
    <title>Display</title>
    <figure>
      <title>The section Display</title>
      <media type="image" mime="image/png" src="figures/machine-display.png">
        <p>The section Display of vm1: No display, Standard VGA, VNC and SDL window, off, and VNC display, 1, greyed; Keyboard layout, empty, at the bottom.</p>
      </media>
    </figure>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>No display</gui></title>
        <p>The machine has no screen at all: only its console, and its serial port. <gui>VNC</gui> and <gui>SDL window</gui> are then greyed. Off by default.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Standard VGA</gui></title>
        <p>A standard VGA card in place of the one of the machine. Off by default.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>VNC</gui>, <gui>VNC display</gui></title>
        <p>Show the screen of the machine over VNC, on the display of that number, from 0 to 500; 1 by default. A VNC viewer connects to port 5900 and the number, as <cmd>vncviewer localhost:1</cmd>. Off by default.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>SDL window</gui></title>
        <p>Show the screen in a window of SDL. Off by default.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Keyboard layout</gui></title>
        <p>The layout of the keyboard of the machine, two letters, as <code>it</code> or <code>de</code>; empty for that of QEMU.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
  </section>
  <section id="sound">
    <title>Sound and USB</title>
    <figure>
      <title>The section Sound and USB</title>
      <media type="image" mime="image/png" src="figures/machine-sound.png">
        <p>The section Sound and USB of vm1: Sound card, None; Audio driver, alsa; USB, off; and under USB devices, greyed, 1d6b:0002, Linux Foundation 2.0 root hub, and 0781:5567, SanDisk Corp. Cruzer Blade, each with its switch.</p>
      </media>
    </figure>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Sound card</gui></title>
        <p><gui>None</gui>, the default; <gui>PC speaker</gui>; or one of the sound cards of this QEMU.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Audio driver</gui></title>
        <p>Where the sound goes on this computer, as the <gui>Settings</gui> window says: see <link xref="settings#computer"/>.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>USB</gui></title>
        <p>Give the machine USB, and the devices below. Off by default.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>USB devices</gui></title>
        <p>The USB devices of this computer, each with a switch that gives it to the machine. A device that the machine has and this computer hasn't shows as not on this host. QEMU runs as you: it needs the right to open the device.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
  </section>
  <section id="kernel">
    <title>Kernel</title>
    <figure>
      <title>The section Kernel</title>
      <media type="image" mime="image/png" src="figures/machine-kernel.png">
        <p>The section Kernel of vm1: Boot a kernel, off, so Kernel, Initial ramdisk, Ramdisk and Command line are greyed; under Debugging, GDB, off.</p>
      </media>
    </figure>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Boot a kernel</gui></title>
        <p>Boot a kernel of your own, not the one on the disks. Off by default.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Kernel</gui></title>
        <p>With <gui>Boot a kernel</gui>: the file of the kernel.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Initial ramdisk</gui>, <gui>Ramdisk</gui></title>
        <p>With <gui>Boot a kernel</gui>: load an initial ramdisk with the kernel, and its file.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Command line</gui></title>
        <p>The command line of the kernel, as <code>console=ttyS0 root=/dev/vda1</code>.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>GDB</gui>, <gui>GDB port</gui></title>
        <p>Let GDB debug the machine, on that TCP port, from 1 to 65535; 1234 by default. In GDB, <cmd>target remote localhost:1234</cmd>. Off by default.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
  </section>
  <section id="advanced">
    <title>Advanced</title>
    <figure>
      <title>The section Advanced</title>
      <media type="image" mime="image/png" src="figures/machine-advanced.png">
        <p>The section Advanced of vm1: Local time, Drift fix and Serial socket, off; Icon, empty, with the button that chooses a file.</p>
      </media>
    </figure>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Local time</gui></title>
        <p>Start the clock of the machine at the local time, not UTC, as Windows expects. Off by default.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Drift fix</gui></title>
        <p>Correct the drift of the clock of the machine, which a busy computer slows down. Off by default.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Serial socket</gui></title>
        <p>Connect the serial port of the machine to a socket, <file>vm1_serial</file> for <sys>vm1</sys>, in the folder of the sockets of the project, see <link xref="config-files">virtualbricks-config(5)</link>; a program such as <cmd>socat</cmd> connects to it. Off by default.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Icon</gui></title>
        <p>A picture to show in place of the icon of a virtual machine, in the list and in the <gui>Topology</gui> tab.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
  </section>
  <section id="console">
    <title>The console of a machine</title>
    <p>While the machine runs, <guiseq><gui>Process</gui><gui>Open Control Monitor</gui></guiseq>, in its menu, opens the monitor of QEMU in a terminal, the one of the <gui>Terminal</gui> setting. There you type the commands of QEMU, as <cmd>info network</cmd>.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="state">
    <title>Stop, suspend, resume</title>
    <p><gui>Stop</gui> asks the system of the machine to shut down. <gui>Suspend</gui>, under <gui>Process</gui>, saves the state of the machine in its first disk and stops it; <gui>Resume</gui> starts it again from there. <gui>Reset</gui> is the reset button of the machine.</p>
  </section>
</page>
